<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William V, Prince of Orange</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William V, Prince of Orange, was the last stadtholder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, holding the office from 1751, as a child, until 1795. His weak rule and the defeats of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-dutch-war">Fourth Anglo-Dutch War</a> fed the Patriot movement, which challenged his powers until a Prussian invasion restored him in 1787. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-occupation-of-the-netherlands">French revolutionary armies</a> conquered the Republic in 1795 he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-v-prince-of-orange.jpg" alt="William V, Prince of Orange" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Bone / After John Hoppner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_V,_Prince_of_Orange_-_Bone_1801.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William V was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> on 8 March 1748, the only son of William IV, who had been restored as stadtholder of the United Provinces the year before. He was three years old when his father died in 1751, and a long regency began, held by his mother Anne, his grandmother Marie Louise, Duke Louis Ernest of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> and his sister Carolina. He took up the office of stadtholder and Captain-General of the Dutch States Army on his majority in 1766. By the secret Acte van Consulentschap he let the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> keep a large influence on the government. In 1767 he married Princess Wilhelmina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> were neutral in the American War of Independence, and William, leading the pro-British faction, blocked attempts to bring the Republic into the war. The Dutch attempt to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-armed-neutrality">League of Armed Neutrality</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-dutch-war">Fourth Anglo-Dutch War</a> in 1780. The war went badly. The Dutch lost Sint Eustatius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagapattinam">Nagapattinam</a>, scandals such as the Brest Affair undermined belief in the navy, and the regime and the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> were suspected of treason. The pamphlet Aan het Volk van Nederland, published in 1781 by Joan van der Capellen tot den Pol, called for political reform.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1783 the Patriots, a coalition of old regenten and democrats, challenged him more and more. In 1785 he moved his court to Het Loo, and in September 1786 sent troops to overthrow the Patriot councils of towns, whereupon the States of Holland deprived him of his command of the army. In June 1787 his wife Wilhelmina was stopped on her way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> by the Gouda Free Corps. Her brother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-ii-of-prussia">Frederick William II of Prussia</a>, took this as an insult and an excuse, and invaded Holland in September 1787 to suppress the Patriots.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>William joined the First Coalition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1793. In 1795, with the French Army supporting the revolutionaries, he went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. A few days later the Batavian Revolution replaced the Republic with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a>. From Kew he wrote letters to the governors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonies telling them to hand the colonies to the British, which only some did; the British later returned most of them, but kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>. He died in exile at his daughter's palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, the last of the Dutch stadtholders. His son William VI returned in 1813 and proclaimed himself king, the first Dutch monarch of the House of Orange.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William V, Prince of Orange” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Defeat fed the Patriot movement, which demanded reform and attacked the powers of the stadtholder <b>William V</b>. When the Patriots gained control of several towns and provinces, Prussia invaded in 1787 to restore William V, whose wife Wilhelmina was the Prussian king’s sister, and many Patriots fled to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in Brunswick, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.08,4.31&amp;year=1785" aria-label="The Hague on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William V, Prince of Orange"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5226 5668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5226 5668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.08,4.31&amp;year=1785">What was here in 1785? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
